A||||3||Tom88gt||Fiero gets butt whipped at SCCA event||||11||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||02-28-2000||04:18 PM||||Just finished autocrossing my Fiero at the Solo II national tour at Meridian.  It was only my second time autocrossing and first time in the Fiero (only had the car 2 months, 100% stock).  <P>I felt a bit outclassed by the competition.  My entire class was second generation MR2s ('91+) and MX5 Miatas ('90+ 1.6L).  It seems that the SCCA organization must think highly of the 1980's heavy V-6 Fiero to put it in C Stock with the 1990's overhead cam, lighter, better balanced, more performace parts available Jap cars.  Everyone else had K&N filters and most had exhaust mods and Konis.  The other drivers were shocked to find out how heavy the Fiero is and that it only had 135 hp.<P>Actually, the other drivers had several years experience.  Also, the other 2 cars who finnished just ahead of me in dead last were running street tires also.  I guess I better practice a lot and move up to racing tires.  My best run was 64 seconds compared to the winner's MR2 at 50 seconds.  A big difference but there were about 10-15 cars in the class (I didn't count).<P>Another Fiero showed up from Alabama.  He got stuck in E Modified because he had an engine swap, 3.4 TDC from the Fiero Factory, which was obvious even to someone who had never seen a Fiero engine bay before.  Still a very good conversion, just not stealth.  It have been more fair to put him in A Stock with the Turbo MR2s.  <P>If more Fieros would join and race in SCCA, they might have better data and history on them to better categorize the Fiero.  For example, one guy had his '72 911 in A Stock racing against brand new Boxters and Carreras.  Is that fair?  Sounds like 28 years of technological improvements don't mean shit.    ||||reg||11
Z||000001||Greg Piet||02-28-2000||04:29 PM||||Well You are scaring me!  My first SOLOII is on sunday, and I thought I would make a fool of myself but I dont want to be completely laughed at.   One note is that the guy who got me started in the SCCA drives the Fiero in the C (SP) class.  He got the best times in the C class for the past 5 races.  I think there were about 15 other c class cars as well.  His wife also races the car, and she rates at about the middle of the pack.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||02-28-2000||04:55 PM||||Don't worry.  There are only 2 types of people who show up at SCCA events.  Those who are fast, and those who think they are fast.  And there are only 2 types who leave. Those who know they are fast, and those who who are not fast, but faster than when they came.  It's all fun and games.<P>Every event is a learning experience.  At the local events you can have a pax.  Have a buddy who knows SCCA ride with you and then evaluate your performance.<P>Your skill, or lack of it, is the biggest factor.  I saw Yugos with faster times than ZR1 Vettes.<P>When I get really good and start beating Miatas and MR2s, then I will start upgrading the Fiero.||||reg||6
Z||000003||Ed||02-28-2000||08:17 PM||||I saw an autocross one time where a guy who normally runs a Pantera (it was broke and he couldn't run it) had a RENTAL thunderbird. Not an SC or anything special, just a regular u-rent-um Ford.  He had really good times! So, take heart auto-Xer's, From what I've seen here and at other races, skill is at least as important as equipment, maybe more so.  ||||reg||1
